"""Australia flagship course content (7). Own wording."""
from flagship_nl_sg_au import M
from flagship_data_nl import K, SAFE, O4
C = []
def S(id, title, summary, task, cond, std, outs, jobs, mods, safety=SAFE):
    C.append(K(id, title, summary, task, cond, std, safety, outs, jobs, mods))
S('AU-01','Financial Services and Fintech','Understand how money products work, then design a small digital money tool on paper and test it with practice data. Throughout, you check the tool for fairness, privacy and fraud risk, and you learn when a licensed adviser must be involved.',
 'Design and test a simple money tool (a budget, a payment reminder or a savings tracker) using practice data only. Finish with a needs statement, a prototype, a fault log, a fee table and a privacy and fairness review checked by a licensed adviser.','About 140 hours over 3 to 6 months. You need a computer, a free spreadsheet program, practice data only (never real accounts), a licensed adviser for the review step, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Needs statement based on five interviews; flow design with three screens or sheets; practice-data test of ten cases with a fault log; fee and fairness table; privacy note with adviser comment; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain how payments, savings and credit work, including timing and fees.','Build and test a simple money tool using practice data.','Analyse cost, risk and fairness for different kinds of users.','Create a clear customer guide in plain words.'),
 ['Financial services assistant','Customer support analyst','Fintech operations assistant','Product tester'],
 [M('How money moves','Understand',12,'Every payment travels through several parties before it arrives, and each step adds time, cost and a chance of error. Knowing the path helps you explain delays to a customer and design tools that set honest expectations.','A payment path has four parts: the payer, the payer bank, a clearing network and the payee bank.|Instant payment types can arrive in seconds at any hour, but batch bank transfers use cut-off times, so a batch transfer sent after the cut-off is processed on the next business day.|Weekends and public holidays do not count as business days, which adds one or more days to the wait.|A fee can be charged to the payer, the payee or split between them, so always ask who pays.|Common errors are wrong account numbers, duplicate payments and mismatched references, and each needs a clear way to be reported and fixed.','A batch bank transfer (not an instant payment) of A$250 is sent at 4:45 pm on Friday and the bank cut-off is 3:30 pm. It is processed on Monday, so the payee sees the money on Monday, 3 days after the customer pressed send. If an A$2 fee is charged to the payer, the payer is debited A$252 and the payee receives A$250.','Draw a labelled payment path for one transfer, showing each party, the time it takes at each step, any fee, and one place where an error could happen.','The path shows at least four labelled steps, one cut-off time, one fee and one error point, and a reader can follow it without help.','Why can a transfer take longer than the customer expects?','Batch bank transfers use cut-off times and checks, and weekends and holidays do not count as business days, so a late request waits for the next processing run (instant payment types are faster).'),
  M('Savings, credit and interest','Understand',16,'Interest is the price of using money over time. Savers earn it and borrowers pay it, and comparing offers fairly means using the same time period and the same method every time.','Simple interest is calculated on the starting amount only: interest = principal x rate x years.|Compound interest is calculated on the balance including past interest, so it grows faster each year.|The cost of credit includes interest, fees and any penalty for late payment, not just the headline rate.|To compare two offers, convert both to the same period, such as the total paid over 12 months.|A common mistake is to compare a monthly rate with an annual rate without converting one of them.','Save A$1,000 at 5% for 2 years. Simple interest is 1,000 x 0.05 x 2 = A$100, so the total is A$1,100. Compound interest is 1,000 x 1.05 x 1.05 = A$1,102.50, so compounding adds A$2.50 more over two years.','Calculate simple and compound interest for three cases of your own, with different amounts, rates and years, and show the working for each.','All three cases are correct to the cent, written with the currency sign and the number of years.','What is the compound total on A$1,000 at 5% for 2 years, and how does it differ from simple interest?','The compound total is A$1,102.50, which is A$2.50 higher than the simple total of A$1,100 because the second year earns interest on the first year interest.'),
  M('Know the customer','Analyse',16,'A money tool only helps if it fits the way real people earn and spend. You start by listening, not by building, so you design for a real problem instead of a guess.','Interview five people who might use the tool and ask them to describe the last time money felt hard to manage.|Write down each answer in the speaker words, then group similar answers under headings.|List each person goals and fears, such as wanting to avoid overdraft fees or fearing hidden charges.|Count how many of the five share each problem and pick the one that appears most often.|Write one needs statement in the form: this person needs a way to do this, so that this result happens.','Of five interviewed casual workers, four say their pay changes each week and they cannot plan bills. The needs statement reads: casual workers need a weekly budget that updates when pay changes, so that they can set aside rent before spending.','Write a one-paragraph needs statement based on your five interviews, and attach a table showing how many people raised each problem.','The statement names a specific user, a specific problem and a specific result, and the table shows a count out of five for each problem.','Why interview people before designing the tool?','Tools only work if they fit real money habits, and interviews show the actual problem instead of an assumption.'),
  M('Design the tool','Create',20,'A good money tool is simple, shows costs early and lets people change their mind. You build it on paper or in a spreadsheet first, because mistakes are cheap to fix there.','Limit the tool to three screens or sheets, such as input, summary and help.|Use plain words and avoid terms like amortisation unless you explain them in one short line.|Show every fee and the total cost before the user confirms an action.|Include an undo or cancel step and a help line that says where to get human support.|Test the draft with two people who have not seen it and note where they hesitate or ask questions.','A savings reminder tool shows a screen that reads: this reminder costs A$0 to set up and a late-payment alert costs nothing. The confirm button stays disabled until the user ticks that they have read the fee line, and a cancel link sits beside it.','Build a prototype of your tool with three screens or sheets, including a fee line before confirmation and a cancel option.','A tester completes the main task without help, and the fee is visible before the confirm step.','Why must fees appear before the user confirms an action?','The user can only give fair consent if they know the full cost beforehand, and hiding it causes complaints and harm.'),
  M('Test with practice data','Apply',24,'Testing finds faults before a user does. You use made-up data only, so no real person or account is ever put at risk.','Create a practice data set of at least ten made-up people with names, balances and dates that do not match anyone real.|Write ten test cases that include normal use, empty entries, very large amounts and a negative number.|Run each case, record what you expected and what happened, and mark pass or fail.|Log each fault with a short description, the steps to repeat it and how serious it is.|Fix the most serious fault, rerun the same case and record that it now passes.','Test case 7 enters A$19.99 split between three people. The tool shows A$6.66 each, which totals A$19.98, so one cent is lost. The fault is logged as a rounding error and fixed by giving the extra cent to the first person.','Run ten test cases on your prototype with practice data and produce a fault log listing each fault, its severity and its fix.','The log has ten cases with expected and actual results, at least one fault found and one fault fixed and retested.','Why use practice data and never real money or accounts?','Real data can cause harm and privacy breaches, and practice data lets you break things safely.'),
  M('Rules, privacy and fraud','Apply',20,'Money tools are regulated, and rules differ between places and between types of service. Your job is to spot where rules may apply, collect as little data as possible and plan for fraud, then ask a qualified person to confirm.','List what the tool does, such as holding money, giving advice or lending, because each may need a licence.|Check with your local authority and a licensed adviser before any real use, since rules and penalties vary.|Collect only the data the tool needs and delete it when it is no longer needed.|Add basic fraud checks such as a second confirmation for large or unusual actions.|Write a one-page privacy note saying what you collect, why, who sees it and how long you keep it.','A tool that sends payment reminders needs only a name, a due date and an amount. It does not need a birth date. The design adds a two-step check, so any change to an amount above A$1,000 needs a code sent to the user before it is saved.','Write a one-page note covering licence questions, data collected, fraud checks and the adviser comment on each.','The note lists at least three data items with reasons, two fraud checks, and a written comment from a licensed adviser.','Why involve a licensed adviser before real use?','Rules and licences vary by place and by service, and breaking them can bring penalties for you and harm for users.'),
  M('Fairness and cost','Evaluate',16,'A tool can follow every rule and still treat some users unfairly. Evaluating means asking who gains, who pays and whether people with less money pay more.','Build a fee table that lists every charge, when it applies and the maximum a user could pay in a month.|Compare the monthly cost for a user with A$500 and a user with A$5,000 to see who pays the larger share.|Check whether penalties such as late fees fall hardest on people with irregular income.|Consider a cap on fees so a single mistake does not cost more than the benefit of the tool.|Write a plain summary a user could read in under one minute.','A late fee of A$10 is 2% of a A$500 balance but only 0.2% of a A$5,000 balance, so the fee hurts the smaller balance ten times more. Capping late fees at A$10 per month and waiving the first one protects low-income users.','Build a fee table and write a short summary of the effect on low-income users with one suggested change.','The table lists every fee with a monthly maximum, and the summary names a clear group and a clear change.','Name one check you can use to test fairness.','Compare the cost as a share of balance for a low-income user and a higher-income user, and check whether penalties hit irregular earners hardest.'),
  M('Guide and review','Create',16,'A customer guide turns your work into something real people can use. The review shows what you learned and what you would change next.','Write a one-page guide with a short title, three to five numbered steps and one example with real numbers.|Include a section called what it costs and a section called where to get help.|Read the guide aloud to a friend and change any word they do not understand.|List three lessons from the course, each tied to one thing you did.|Write next steps with a date, an owner and a measure for each.','The guide has a title: how to track weekly savings. Step 2 reads: enter this week savings, for example A$40, and the sheet adds it to the total. A fee section says there are no charges and a help line names a free money advice service in your area.','Present your one-page guide and your three lessons and next steps to your mentor or peer circle.','The guide has steps, a worked example, a cost section and a help section, and the reviewer gives written feedback.','What should a good customer guide contain?','Clear steps with an example, the full cost, and where to get help.')],
 safety="Learning only. Use practice data. Do not handle real accounts, lend, advise or move money unless licensed and permitted. Check with your local authority about licensing and consumer rules before any real use.")
S('AU-02','Pharma and Life Sciences Operations','Learn how medicines and health products move from development to patient. The course focuses on accurate quality records, safe storage, tracing batches and explaining risks clearly, all with practice documents.',
 'Complete a quality-record exercise with ten practice records, a cold-chain log review, a deviation analysis and a plain-language safety summary using practice documents only.','About 140 hours over 3 to 6 months. You need practice documents, a free spreadsheet program, a qualified adviser for review, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Ten practice records complete with no gaps; cold-chain log reviewed with a recorded decision; three deviation reports with cause and action; safety summary at a plain reading level; ethics note; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain the path from development to patient and why each stage has checks.','Complete records and cold-chain checks accurately.','Analyse deviations to find causes and prevent repeats.','Write a plain-language safety summary.'),
 ['Quality assistant','Documentation assistant','Supply chain assistant','Regulatory support assistant'],
 [M('Development to patient','Understand',12,'A medicine takes many years and many checks to reach a patient. Each stage exists to answer a question about safety, quality or benefit, and each has someone responsible for the answer.','The main stages are discovery, laboratory testing, human trials, approval, manufacture and supply, and monitoring after release.|Human trials usually run in phases, starting with a small group to check safety and growing to larger groups to check benefit.|Approval means an independent authority reviews the evidence before the product can be supplied.|Manufacture follows written procedures so every batch matches the approved quality.|After release, reports of side effects are collected so new risks can be found and acted on.','A trial site must have approval before the first person is enrolled. If a plan to enrol 60 people is split into three groups of 20, each group is checked for safety before the next group starts, so a problem is found with 20 people, not 60.','Draw the path from discovery to monitoring, naming each stage, what question it answers and who is responsible.','All six stages are named in order, each with one question and one responsible role.','Why must trials come before general supply?','Trials test safety and benefit on small groups first so problems are found before many people are exposed.'),
  M('Quality records','Apply',20,'If it is not written down, it did not happen. Good records are clear, complete and dated, and they let anyone understand what was done and by whom.','Record who did the task, what was done, when it was done and the result, using the same format every time.|Write in permanent ink or in a system that tracks changes, and never use correction fluid.|Correct a mistake by drawing one line through it, writing the right value, then adding your initials and the date.|Never leave a blank field; write not applicable or a dash if nothing belongs there.|Have a second person check each record against the procedure before it is filed.','A record shows a weight entered as 12.5 g but the true value was 15.2 g. The writer draws one line through 12.5, writes 15.2, and adds the initials and the date next to it. The original stays readable, so the change can be checked.','Complete ten practice records using the correct format and correct at least two mistakes the right way.','All ten records have no blank fields, every correction is lined through, initialled and dated, and a second person signs each one.','How do you correct a mistake in a quality record?','Draw one line through it, write the correct entry, and add your initials and the date so the original is still readable.'),
  M('Batch and traceability','Apply',16,'Traceability means you can say exactly where each batch went. If a problem is found, quick tracing limits harm and makes a recall faster.','Every batch has a unique number printed on the label and recorded on each document.|Keep a distribution list showing which customers received which batch and how many units.|Record every handover so the chain of custody shows who held the product at each step.|Run a mock recall to test whether you can find all units of one batch within a set time.|Check that the number of units found plus those still in stock equals the number produced.','A batch of 2,400 units is traced in a mock recall. The distribution list shows 1,900 units sent to 8 customers and 500 units in stock. Since 1,900 + 500 = 2,400, all units are accounted for, and the team finishes in 1 hour 40 minutes, inside the 2-hour target.','Complete a timed trace exercise on a practice batch and write a one-page result with the time taken and any units missing.','The trace is finished inside two hours, and units found plus units in stock equal units produced.','Why does every batch have a number?','The number lets you trace where each unit went and remove affected units quickly in a recall.'),
  M('Cold chain and storage','Apply',20,'Many health products must stay in a set temperature range. A reading outside the range is an excursion, and the response depends on how far and how long it went.','Know the allowed range on the label, for example 2 C to 8 C for refrigerated products.|Use calibrated sensors and log readings at set times, such as every 4 hours, plus a continuous recorder where possible.|Treat any reading outside the range as an excursion and write down the start time, end time and highest or lowest reading.|Isolate the product, tell the responsible person and do not use or ship it until a decision is recorded.|Keep calibration certificates and check that each sensor is within its calibration date.','A fridge limit is 2 C to 8 C. The log shows 9.5 C for 3 hours overnight. That is an excursion of 1.5 C above the limit for 3 hours, so the stock is quarantined and sent for review, and the decision with a signature is written in the log.','Review a practice temperature log, mark every excursion and record a decision for each one.','Every excursion is found, with start, end, peak and a signed decision for each.','What is an excursion?','A reading outside the allowed temperature range, which must be recorded and reviewed before the product is used.'),
  M('Deviations and improvement','Analyse',20,'A deviation is anything that did not go as written. Handling deviations well means finding the real cause, fixing the problem and stopping it from returning.','Describe the event with facts only: what happened, when, where and who found it.|Ask why at least three times to move from the symptom to the root cause.|Record an immediate fix and a longer-term action that prevents repeats.|Judge the impact on product quality and patient safety, and decide who must be told.|Review deviations monthly to spot trends, such as the same fault on the same shift.','Three batch records in one month lack a signature. Asking why shows the signing step is not on the checklist. The fix is to add a signature box and a final check; the count of missing signatures is tracked, and falls from 3 in month one to 0 in month two.','Analyse three practice deviations and write a report for each with description, cause, fix and prevention.','Each report has four labelled parts, a cause that goes deeper than the symptom and an action with an owner and a date.','What are the four parts of a deviation report?','Describe the event, find the cause, fix it, and prevent it from happening again.'),
  M('Safety communication','Create',20,'People who take medicines need to understand the risks without a medical degree. Plain language is a safety tool, not a style choice.','Write short sentences of about 15 words or fewer and use everyday words, such as stomach pain, not abdominal pain.|State the benefit and the main risks side by side and include how often each risk happens, for example 1 in 100.|Avoid claims that cannot be shown, such as completely safe or no side effects.|Tell the reader what to do if a problem happens and who to contact.|Test the text with a reader unfamiliar with the topic and aim for a reading level of about Year 8.','Instead of: hypotension may occur in some patients, write: your blood pressure may drop and you may feel dizzy. This happens to about 2 in 100 people. If you feel faint, sit down and call your health service.','Write a one-page plain-language summary for a practice product and test it with one reader.','The summary uses short sentences, gives a number for each risk, includes what to do and the reader can explain it back.','Why is plain language important for safety information?','Patients must understand the risks and what to do, or they cannot use the product safely.'),
  M('Ethics and consent','Evaluate',16,'People in trials and people using products trust the system to protect them. Ethics here means consent, privacy and honest reporting, even when the truth is inconvenient.','Informed consent means a person is told the purpose, risks and their right to leave, and agrees freely without pressure.|Keep personal and health data private, share it only with people who need it and remove names where you can.|Report every adverse event honestly and quickly, even if it seems minor or unrelated.|Never change or hide a result to make a product look better.|Know who to report concerns to, and check with your local authority for the rules in your area.','During a practice exercise, a participant reports a headache two hours after a dose. The team writes it as an adverse event within one working day, even though it may be unrelated, so the safety team can look for a pattern across all participants.','Write a one-page ethics note covering consent, privacy and reporting for a practice scenario.','The note names the three principles, gives one example of each and says who to report to.','Why must adverse events be reported even when they seem unrelated?','A single report may look minor but a pattern across reports can reveal a real risk and protect patients.'),
  M('Review','Evaluate',16,'The review is where you turn practice into lasting skill. You compare your work with the standard and decide what to improve first.','List three lessons, each linked to a specific record, log or report you produced.|Rate your own work against the standard for each exercise and note the biggest gap.|Create a short plan with one action, an owner, a date and a measure for each gap.|Share your work with a peer and invite one written comment.|Update your checklist or template so the lesson is built into the next piece of work.','You find three of ten records had a missing date. The plan is to add a date check to the final review, with the aim of zero missing dates in the next ten records by the end of next month, and the new checklist is shared with your peer.','Present your three lessons, your plan and one updated checklist to your mentor or peer circle.','The plan has at least two actions with a date and a measure, and the checklist includes at least one new check.','What should you do after finding a gap in your work?','Write a specific action with a date and a measure, and add a check to your checklist so the gap is less likely to return.')],
 safety="Learning only. Use practice documents. Never handle, advise on or supply medicines unless qualified and permitted. Check with your local authority about the rules for medicines and health products in your area.")
S('AU-03','Data Analysis and Data Science Practice','Ask a clear question, find and clean data, describe and chart it, test claims and report honestly. You practise on public or practice data and learn to state limits openly.',
 'Answer one real question using a public or practice dataset. Produce a cleaned dataset with a change log, a summary of the numbers, two honest charts, a test of one claim and a one-page report that states limits.','About 150 hours over 3 to 6 months. You need a computer, a free spreadsheet program and notebook tools, practice data, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Clear measurable question; data dictionary; cleaned data with change log and raw copy kept; summary statistics; two charts with labelled units; one tested claim; one-page report with limits; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain how a question, data and a decision connect.','Clean and describe a dataset with a full change log.','Test whether a pattern is real or due to chance.','Create a clear chart and an honest report.'),
 ['Data analyst assistant','Reporting analyst','Junior data scientist','Research assistant'],
 [M('Ask a good question','Understand',12,'A clear question saves hours of wandering through data. It names who will use the answer, what they will decide and how the result is measured.','Start by naming the person who will use the answer and the decision they will make.|Make the question measurable by naming the exact number, such as average daily sales in dollars.|State the time period and place, such as the last 12 months in one region.|Write three possible questions and rank them by how useful and how answerable they are.|Check that the data you need exists before you commit to the question.','A cafe owner asks: do weekday sales fall in the 3 days after a promotion ends? That is measurable, has a time frame and links to a decision about when to run the next promotion. The vague version, how are sales going, could not be answered.','Write three candidate questions, rank them and choose one, giving a reason for the choice.','The chosen question names a user, a decision, a measure and a time period.','Name one trait of a good analysis question.','It is measurable and tied to a decision someone needs to make.'),
  M('Get and understand data','Apply',16,'Before analysing anything you must know what each column means, where the data came from and what is missing. Skipping this step is the most common cause of wrong answers.','Record the source, the date it was collected and the date you downloaded it.|Write a data dictionary listing each column, its meaning, its unit and its type, such as number, date or text.|Check units carefully, because a column mixing dollars and cents or kilograms and grams gives wrong totals.|Count missing values in every column and find how they are coded, for example blank, zero or 999.|Look at the first 20 rows and the last 20 rows to spot odd entries.','A sales table has 400 rows. The age column has 37 entries of 999, which is a code for unknown, not a real age. If you included them the average age of 400 customers would be inflated, so they must be treated as missing.','Describe your dataset with a data dictionary and a count of missing values for each column.','Every column has a meaning, a unit and a missing count, and any coded values are identified.','Why must you check units before analysing?','Mixed units, such as dollars and cents, give wrong totals and wrong averages.'),
  M('Clean data','Apply',24,'Cleaning makes the data fit for analysis without secretly changing the story. The rule is to keep the raw copy untouched and record every change.','Save a read-only raw copy first and work only on a second copy.|Find and remove exact duplicate rows, and record how many you removed.|Fix obvious entry errors, such as 2024 typed as 2042, only when you can show why it is wrong.|Decide how to treat missing values, either remove the rows, fill with a stated value or leave them blank, and write down the reason.|Keep a change log with the date, the action, the number of rows affected and the reason.','A file starts with 412 rows. After removing 12 duplicates it has 400 rows. The change log reads: removed 12 exact duplicate rows, 412 to 400, because the same order was entered twice on import. The raw file remains saved unchanged.','Clean your dataset and submit the cleaned file, the raw file and a change log with at least five entries.','Row counts before and after each step match, and the raw file is unchanged.','Why keep an untouched raw copy of the data?','So you can check or undo any change and show how you reached your results.'),
  M('Describe','Analyse',20,'Summary numbers show the centre and spread of the data. Choosing the right summary stops a few extreme values from misleading you.','The mean is the sum divided by the count, and it is pulled by very large or very small values.|The median is the middle value when sorted, and it is steadier when data is skewed.|Spread can be shown with the minimum, maximum and range, or with the middle half of values.|When comparing groups, report counts alongside percentages so readers can see how big each group is.|Group the data by one category at a time and compare the same measure.','Five households spend A$20, A$22, A$25, A$30 and A$163 on a weekly item. The mean is (20+22+25+30+163) / 5 = A$52, but the median is A$25, which describes the typical household better because one very high value pulls the mean up.','Compute the mean, median, minimum and maximum for two columns and compare two groups with counts and percentages.','All numbers are correct, and you explain in one sentence which summary suits each column.','When is the median a better summary than the mean?','When the data is skewed or has extreme values, because the median is not pulled by them.'),
  M('Visualise','Create',20,'A chart should answer your question at a glance. The chart type, the labels and the axis starting point decide whether it informs or misleads.','Use a bar chart to compare categories and a line chart to show change over time.|Label both axes with the measure and the unit, and give the chart a title that states the finding.|Start a bar chart axis at zero, because a cut axis makes small differences look huge.|Use few colours and use the same colour for the same thing across charts.|Test the chart on someone new and check they can state the main point in about 10 seconds.','A bar chart compares weekly sales of A$4,200 and A$4,500 for two stores. With the axis starting at zero the bars look similar. If the axis starts at A$4,000 one bar looks two and a half times taller, which is misleading, so zero is used and the title reads: store B sold 7% more.','Create two charts, one for categories and one for time, each with a clear title, labelled axes and units.','A reader states the main point of each chart in 10 seconds, and the axes start honestly.','Why must every chart axis have a label and a unit?','Readers cannot interpret the numbers without knowing what is measured and in which unit.'),
  M('Test claims','Analyse',24,'A pattern in a sample might be real or might be chance. Testing means checking size, fairness of comparison and alternative explanations before you claim anything.','Check the sample size, because a pattern in 8 cases is much weaker than the same pattern in 800.|Compare like with like, for example the same weeks of the year or the same type of customer.|Calculate the difference between groups and note how large it is relative to normal variation.|Remember that two things rising together does not show one causes the other.|Look for a third factor that could explain both, such as hot weather.','Ice cream sales and sunburn cases both rise in summer, but ice cream does not cause sunburn; hot sunny weather drives both. If group A averages 52 and group B averages 48 in samples of only 6 each, a 4-point gap could easily be chance.','Choose one claim from your dataset and test it, writing a short note on sample size, fair comparison and other explanations.','The note states the sample sizes, the size of the difference and at least one alternative explanation.','Does a correlation between two things prove one causes the other?','No, a third factor or chance could explain the link, so you need further evidence.'),
  M('Ethics and privacy','Evaluate',16,'Data about people can harm them if handled carelessly. Ethical analysis protects individuals and checks that results are fair to groups.','Remove names, addresses and other identifiers before sharing or publishing any data.|Check small groups, because a group of 3 people in a table can make someone identifiable.|Compare results across groups such as age bands or regions to see whether one group is treated differently.|Use data only for the purpose it was collected for unless people agreed to more.|Check with your local authority for the privacy rules in your area before using data about people.','A table shows average spend by suburb, and one suburb has only 2 customers. Publishing it could reveal their spending, so the 2 are merged into a larger region of 31 customers. A bias check shows approval rates are 70% for one age group and 52% for another, which is flagged for review.','Write a one-page note covering identifiers removed, small groups handled and one bias check on your data.','The note lists the steps taken, shows one group comparison with numbers and names any remaining risk.','Name one check for bias in data.','Compare the results across groups, such as age bands, and look for large unexplained gaps.'),
  M('Report','Create',18,'A report is useful only if the reader can find the answer quickly and trust how you got it. Lead with the answer, then show the evidence and the limits.','Put the answer in the first sentence, written in plain words with the key number.|Follow with the evidence, using one or two charts and a short table.|State limits openly, such as a small sample, missing values or a short time period.|Finish with a recommended next step and who should take it.|List the steps in order so another person could repeat your work from the raw file.','Report opening: weekday sales fall 11% in the 3 days after a promotion, from A$3,200 to A$2,850 a day. Evidence is one chart and one table. Limits: only 6 promotions were recorded and no holiday weeks were checked. Next step: test a promotion with a follow-up offer.','Write and present a one-page report with the answer, evidence, limits, next step and a short list of steps to repeat the work.','The report puts the answer first, shows evidence with units and states at least two limits and one next step.','In what order should a report be laid out?','Answer first, then evidence, limits and next step.')],
 safety="Learning only. Use public or practice data; never publish personal data. Check with your local authority about privacy rules before using data about people.")
S('AU-04','E-commerce and Online Retail Operations','Set up and run a small online store with accurate listings, delivery promises and fair returns. You track results and learn to improve with numbers.',
 'Run a small online store trial for 30 days with a small product range, accurate listings, written policies, orders, delivery and returns, and report results with three proposed changes.','About 130 hours over 3 to 5 months. You need free store tools, a small product range, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Store live with at least 5 listings; delivery, returns and privacy policies written and reviewed; 30-day order and returns record; report with conversion, margin and cost per order; three changes proposed; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain the path from a visit to a repeat sale.','Run orders, delivery and returns for 30 days.','Analyse conversion, margin and cost per order.','Create accurate listings and clear policies.'),
 ['Online store assistant','Order coordinator','Customer service agent','Marketplace seller'],
 [M('Path to purchase','Understand',8,'Every sale begins with a visit and passes through several steps. Seeing where buyers drop out shows you where to improve.','The usual path is visit, view a product, add to basket, enter details, pay and receive the order.|Conversion rate is orders divided by visits, multiplied by 100 to give a percentage.|Buyers leave at points of doubt, such as unexpected delivery costs or unclear returns.|Trust signals include clear photos, real contact details, visible policies and honest reviews.|Track how many people reach each step so you can see the biggest drop.','A store has 100 visits and 4 orders, so conversion is 4 / 100 = 4%. Of those visits, 40 add to basket and 30 start checkout, so the biggest share lost is between 30 checkouts and 4 orders, where 26 of 30 leave (87%), which suggests a problem at payment or delivery cost.','Draw a map of the path from visit to repeat sale and mark where you think most buyers leave, with a reason.','The map has at least five steps, a conversion number for each step you can measure and one suspected drop point.','What is the conversion rate for 4 orders from 100 visits?','4%, because 4 divided by 100 times 100 is 4.'),
  M('Listings','Create',16,'A listing is your shop assistant. It must show the product honestly so the buyer knows what arrives, which reduces returns and complaints.','Use at least three clear photos on a plain background, including one showing size next to a common object.|State the size, weight, material, colour and care instructions in the first lines.|Make only claims you can prove, and avoid words like best or guaranteed unless you can back them up.|Write a title with the product type and key detail so it can be found by search, such as: cotton tote bag, 38 cm wide.|Show a price that includes GST where it applies and state clearly what is and is not included.','A tote bag listing says: cotton canvas, 38 cm wide, 40 cm tall, holds up to 8 kg, machine wash cold. One photo shows the bag beside a hand for size. Because the size is clear, returns for wrong size drop from 6 in 50 orders to 2 in 50 orders.','Write five listings with photos, size, material, care details and an honest claim check.','Each listing has three photos, full measurements and no claim that cannot be proved.','Why are honest claims important in listings?','They set correct expectations, reduce returns and complaints and are often required by consumer rules.'),
  M('Prices and margins','Evaluate',14,'A price that ignores fees can hide a loss. You calculate every cost per item so you know what each sale truly earns.','List product cost, packaging, payment fees, platform fees, delivery paid by you and any GST you must pay.|Payment fees are often a percentage plus a fixed amount per order.|Margin is profit divided by the selling price, shown as a percentage.|Test a price by comparing two options and checking the number of orders at each.|Remember that free delivery is a cost to you unless it is included in the price.','A bag sells for A$40. Costs: product A$18.00, payment fee, assumed to be 2.9% (A$1.16) plus A$0.30 for this example, delivery paid A$7.50, packaging A$1.00. Total cost = A$27.96. Profit = 40 - 27.96 = A$12.04. Margin = 12.04 / 40 = 30.1%. No GST line is used because the shop is assumed not to be registered for GST.','Build a spreadsheet that works out cost, profit and margin per item for three products.','The sheet includes every cost line, the formulas are correct and margin is shown as a percentage for each product.','Which costs must be included when working out margin?','All costs including product, packaging, fees, delivery and tax, not just the product cost.'),
  M('Policies and consumer rules','Apply',14,'Customers trust stores that explain their rules clearly. Consumer rules protect buyers and sellers must follow them whatever the policy says.','Write a delivery policy with timeframes, areas served and what happens if a parcel is late.|Write a returns policy that says how many days, who pays return postage and how refunds are paid.|Write a privacy policy that says what data you collect and why, and keep it short.|Faulty goods and goods that do not match the description usually need to be remedied by law, whatever your policy says.|Check with your local authority and an adviser, since consumer and tax rules vary.','A returns policy says: return unused items within 30 days for a refund to the original payment method. Faulty goods are refunded or replaced at no cost to the customer. A faulty A$40 bag is refunded A$40 plus the return postage of A$8, so the customer pays nothing.','Write delivery, returns and privacy policies of one page or less, and ask an adviser to review them.','The three policies state timeframes and costs clearly and an adviser comment is recorded.','Why are clear policies important?','They build trust, reduce disputes and often meet legal requirements.'),
  M('Orders and delivery','Apply',24,'Fulfilment is where promises are kept or broken. A routine for picking, packing and tracking prevents mistakes and late parcels.','Set a daily cut-off time, such as 2 pm, and state that orders before it ship the same day.|Pick items against the order sheet, check each item and count, then pack with enough padding.|Weigh the parcel and measure it so the postage cost you charge matches what you pay.|Send a tracking link to the customer, which reduces how often they ask where the parcel is.|Fix delays quickly by contacting the carrier and telling the customer before they ask.','Over a week, 35 orders arrive before the 2 pm cut-off and 33 ship the same day, a rate of 33 / 35 = 94%. The 2 missed orders were found to be out of stock after the order, so the team adds a daily stock check each morning.','Run orders for 30 days with a log showing order number, ship date, tracking and any delay with its cause.','The log covers every order, with a same-day ship rate and a cause recorded for each delay.','Why share tracking with the customer?','It reduces enquiries and shows the customer exactly where the parcel is.'),
  M('Returns and service','Apply',16,'A return is a chance to keep a customer and learn what is wrong. A fair and fast process turns a problem into trust.','Inspect each returned item and record the reason in a standard list such as wrong size, faulty or changed mind.|Refund or swap within a stated time, such as 5 working days after the item arrives.|Respond to every customer message within one working day, even if only to say you are looking into it.|Count the reasons each month and look for the most common one.|Change the listing, the packing or the supplier to remove the cause.','In one month there are 50 orders and 8 returns, a return rate of 16%. Five returns say wrong size. After adding a size chart to the listing, the next month has 52 orders and 3 returns, a rate of 5.8%, and the chart cost nothing to make.','Process at least five practice returns and keep a log with reason, action, time taken and a change made.','The log has the reason and the response time for each return and at least one change that targets the most common reason.','Why find the cause of each return?','So you can fix the cause and prevent the same return from happening again.'),
  M('Traffic and measures','Analyse',24,'Numbers show which efforts pay off. You track where visitors come from and what each order costs to win.','Record the source of each visit, such as search, social media, email or direct.|Cost per order equals the money spent on a channel divided by the orders it produced.|Repeat rate equals customers who bought again divided by all customers.|Compare channels using the same period and the same measures.|Put the figures in a table and highlight the channel with the lowest cost per order.','Social posts cost A$40 and bring 5 orders, so cost per order is A$8. An email to past buyers costs A$10 and brings 8 orders, so cost per order is A$1.25. Email is cheaper, so more effort goes to building the list.','Produce a table of visits, orders and cost per order by source for the 30 days, with a short note on what to change.','The table is correct, uses the same period for every source and the note names one change with a reason.','What is the cost per order if you spend A$40 and get 8 orders?','A$5, because 40 divided by 8 is 5.'),
  M('Review','Evaluate',14,'The review turns 30 days of work into decisions. You decide what to keep, change or stop, based on the numbers.','Pull together your key numbers: visits, orders, conversion, margin, return rate and cost per order.|Sort each product and each channel into keep, change or stop.|Set a rule for dropping an item, such as margin under 15% and fewer than 3 sales in 30 days.|Write three lessons and the change you will make for each.|Share the report and invite one question from your mentor or peer circle.','Product C has 2 sales in 30 days and a margin of 11%, so it meets the drop rule and is removed. The money saved on stock, A$180, is moved to product A, which has a margin of 30% and sold 14 units.','Present a report with your numbers, a keep, change or stop list and three proposed changes with expected results.','The report uses at least five numbers, applies a stated rule to each product and includes three changes.','What can decide whether to drop a product?','Low margin together with low sales, based on a rule you set in advance.')])
S('AU-05','Human Resources Practice','Run fair hiring, onboarding and people records for a small team. You work with practice scenarios and learn to communicate clearly, reduce bias and protect privacy.',
 'Complete a fair hiring exercise with a job profile, structured questions and a score sheet, an onboarding plan for the first 30 days and a people-records privacy check for a practice team.','About 120 hours over 3 to 5 months. You need practice scenarios, free document tools, a professional adviser, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Job profile with tasks and fair requirements; fair selection record with scores and reasons; 30-day onboarding plan; people-record privacy check; feedback conversation record; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain fair and lawful people practice.','Run a selection and onboarding exercise.','Analyse bias and how well a person fits the role.','Create a clear job profile.'),
 ['HR assistant','Recruitment coordinator','People operations assistant','Onboarding coordinator'],
 [M('Role needs','Understand',10,'Good hiring starts with the work, not with a wish list. A clear profile attracts the right people and avoids unfair barriers.','List the main tasks and the results expected in the first six months.|Separate must-have skills from nice-to-have skills and keep the must-have list short.|Check each requirement asks only for what the work truly needs, such as a licence only if the job requires driving.|Describe hours, location, pay range and any physical needs honestly.|Ask someone who does the job now to check the profile for accuracy.','A profile for a store assistant first required a degree. The team checks the tasks, which are serving customers, counting stock and using a till, and none needs a degree, so the rule is removed. The applicant pool grows from 12 to 31 people in two weeks.','Write a one-page job profile with tasks, results, must-have skills and a fairness check on each requirement.','Each requirement is linked to a task, and any requirement without a link is removed.','Why check each job requirement?','Each requirement must be needed for the work and must not block people unfairly.'),
  M('Attract and select','Apply',20,'Fair selection means every candidate gets the same chance to show their skills. A set of questions and a score sheet make decisions clearer and easier to explain.','Write five to eight questions that match the tasks and ask them in the same order to every candidate.|Use a score sheet with a scale of 1 to 5 and a short description of what each score means.|Score each answer straight after it is given and write the reason beside the score.|Score the questions independently before discussing with other panel members.|Keep all records for the period required by local rules, and check with your local authority and an adviser.','Candidate A scores 4, 3, 5, 4, 3 on five questions, a total of 19 out of 25, or 76%. Candidate B scores 3, 3, 4, 3, 3, a total of 16 out of 25, or 64%. The reasons recorded beside each score support the difference.','Run a mock interview with a peer, using five questions and a score sheet with a written reason for each score.','Every question is scored with a reason and the total and percentage are calculated correctly.','Why ask every candidate the same questions?','It allows a fair comparison and makes decisions easier to explain.'),
  M('Bias and fairness','Analyse',16,'Everyone has biases that can shape decisions without being noticed. You learn to spot them and put steps in place to reduce them.','Common biases include liking people who are similar to you, judging on first impressions and giving too much weight to one trait.|Remove names, photos and personal details in the first review of applications.|Use a diverse panel when possible so that different views are heard.|Compare the scores given by each panel member and discuss large differences.|Review the outcomes by group over time to see if any group is being filtered out more than expected.','In a review of 40 applications, names are hidden. The shortlist grows from 6 of 40 (15%) from one group to 9 of 40 (22.5%). The team notes that hiding names changed the outcome and keeps the step.','Review five practice applications using a blind first review and write a note on what bias controls you used.','The note names two biases, two controls and one result of the review.','Name one way to reduce bias in hiring.','Hide names and personal details in the first review of applications.'),
  M('Offer and contract basics','Apply',14,'A clear written offer avoids confusion later. Employment rules differ by place, so an adviser must check any real contract.','State the job title, start date, hours, pay rate (which must meet the current minimum wage or award rate), pay period and where the work is done.|Include the notice period for both sides, probation if any and who the person reports to.|List the main duties, and say that the rest is described in the profile.|Give the offer in writing and allow time for questions before signing.|Check with your local authority and a professional adviser, since employment rules vary and carry penalties.','A practice offer reads: part-time store assistant, 24 hours a week, A$28 an hour, paid fortnightly. Weekly pay is 24 x 28 = A$672, so a fortnight is A$1,344 before tax, with superannuation paid by the employer on top at the current legal rate. Notice period is 2 weeks for both sides.','Draft a practice offer letter with all key terms and ask an adviser to review it.','The letter has every key term, the pay calculation is correct and an adviser comment is recorded.','Why use an adviser for contract terms?','Employment rules vary by place and a mistake can bring penalties.'),
  M('Onboarding','Create',16,'The first weeks shape how quickly a new person becomes confident and stays. A plan makes sure nothing is missed and the person feels welcome.','Prepare before day one: workspace, accounts, equipment and a welcome message.|Make a day-one plan with introductions, a tour, safety information and a first small task.|Assign a buddy who answers everyday questions and checks in each week.|Schedule check-ins at the end of week 1, week 2 and day 30 to ask what is going well and what is unclear.|Write the learning goals for the first 30 days with a way to measure each.','The plan for a new store assistant: day 1, tour, safety brief and shadow a colleague for 4 hours; week 1, learn the till with a goal of 10 supervised sales; day 30, check-in. At the day-30 review the person completed 62 sales unsupervised.','Create a 30-day onboarding plan with a day-one schedule, a buddy, three check-in dates and measurable goals.','The plan names dates, a buddy role and at least three measurable goals, and a peer finds no gaps.','Why give a new person a buddy?','A buddy gives a safe place for questions, which speeds up learning and belonging.'),
  M('Records and privacy','Apply',16,'People records hold sensitive information. Protecting them builds trust and prevents harm and legal problems.','List what you hold, such as contracts, bank details, medical notes and performance records, and why you need each.|Limit access so only those who need a record can open it, and lock paper files or use passwords.|Set a retention period for each record type and delete or destroy records when the time ends.|Tell people what you hold about them, and let them see and ask to correct their records where the rules allow.|Check with your local authority and an adviser for the rules in your area.','An audit finds 14 files holding bank details, and 5 staff can open them. Only the payroll officer needs access, so the number of people with access drops from 5 to 1, and the files move to a password-protected folder.','Audit a practice set of records and write a short report on access, storage and retention with two fixes.','The report lists each record type, who has access and a retention period, and recommends two fixes.','Why limit access to people records?','It protects privacy and reduces the risk of misuse or leaks.'),
  M('Feedback and conflict','Apply',16,'Clear and kind feedback helps people improve and stops small problems from growing. You practise saying difficult things calmly and listening carefully.','Describe the facts you saw or heard, such as: the report was handed in 2 days after the deadline.|Explain the impact, such as: the team could not send the invoice on time.|Ask a question or make a request, such as: what would help you meet the next deadline.|Listen without interrupting and repeat back what you heard to check you understood.|Write a short record of the conversation, the agreed action and a follow-up date.','Instead of: you are always late, say: the report on Tuesday arrived 2 days late (fact), so the invoice went out 2 days late (impact). Can we agree on a check-in on Monday to keep it on track (ask)? Follow-up set for the next Friday.','Role-play a feedback conversation with a peer and write a record of the conversation, the action and the follow-up date.','The observer confirms facts, impact and a request were used and the record has an action and a date.','What is a simple structure for giving feedback?','State the facts, explain the impact and then ask or request a change.'),
  M('Review','Evaluate',12,'The review shows what you can do now and what to practise next. It also improves the process for the next hire.','List three lessons, each tied to one exercise you completed.|Compare your selection record and onboarding plan against the standard and note two gaps.|Create an action plan with one step, a date and a measure for each gap.|Update your checklist for the process, such as adding a fairness check before shortlisting.|Share your work with a peer and ask for one written suggestion.','Review finds the score sheet had no written reason for 6 of 25 scores. The plan is to require a reason for every score and have a peer check 5 sheets for completeness by the end of next month, with a target of 100% completion.','Present your lessons, plan and updated checklist to your mentor or peer circle.','The plan has at least two actions with dates and measures, and the checklist has at least one new step.','What should you do after finding a gap in your process?','Write an action with a date and a measure, and add a step to your checklist.')],
 safety="Learning only. Use practice scenarios; never share real personal data. Check with your local authority and an adviser about employment and privacy rules before any real use.")
S('AU-06','Digital Marketing Practice','Plan, run and measure a small and honest marketing campaign for a real small business. You learn to set one goal, speak to one audience and read the numbers fairly.',
 'Run a two-week small campaign for a small business partner, with a measurable goal, a defined audience, honest messages, a content calendar and a results table that shows cost per result and three proposed changes.','About 120 hours over 3 to 5 months. You need free tools, a small business partner, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Written goal with a number and a date; audience description; three honest message drafts; two-week calendar; results table with cost per booking; three changes; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain the link between goal, audience, message and channel.','Run a small campaign for two weeks.','Analyse results and cost per result.','Create honest messages that respect consent.'),
 ['Digital marketing assistant','Content coordinator','Social media assistant','Campaign analyst'],
 [M('Goal and audience','Understand',10,'A campaign needs one clear goal and one clear audience. Without them you cannot choose a message or judge success.','Write one goal with a number and a date, such as 20 bookings in 14 days.|Describe the audience by what they need, where they are and what they already know about the business.|Choose a single main message so the audience can remember it.|Check the goal is realistic by looking at last month results or asking the owner.|Decide how each result will be counted before the campaign starts.','A yoga studio sets the goal: 20 bookings in 14 days. Last month, 12 bookings came from about 600 website visits (a 2% rate). To reach 20 in 14 days at the same rate would need about 1,000 visits, so the plan adds an email to 400 past customers.','Write your goal, audience description and single message, and check the goal against past numbers.','The goal has a number and a date, the audience is described by need and the goal is checked against a past figure.','Why choose one goal for a campaign?','It gives focus and makes results easy to measure.'),
  M('Message and offer','Create',16,'A message tells the audience what they gain and what to do next. It must be clear, honest and easy to act on.','Use the structure benefit, proof and action, such as: relax after work, 40 reviews from local members, book your first class.|Write in everyday words and keep each message to two or three short sentences.|Make only true claims, because false or misleading claims can break advertising law and lose trust.|State the offer clearly, including any limits or end dates.|Write three versions and ask five people which one they understand best.','Message A: Your first class is free. Book before Sunday. Message B: Unwind after work, try a class on us. 4.8 average rating from 62 local members. Book here. Five testers pick B as clearer, and it states a true, checkable fact.','Draft three versions of your main message and test them with five people, recording which was clearest.','All three drafts include benefit, proof and action, and the test result shows a count out of five.','Why avoid false claims in a message?','They break advertising rules and damage trust.'),
  M('Channels','Apply',16,'A channel is where your message reaches people. You choose a small number so you can learn from each.','The main channels are search, social media and email, and each reaches people in a different way.|Pick where your audience already spends time, using the interviews or the owner existing customer list.|Compare cost, effort and speed, because email to existing clients is often cheap and quick.|Choose two channels and set a budget for each, even if the budget is zero.|Write the reason for each choice in one sentence.','A bakery chooses email to 300 existing customers (no cost, 1 hour) and one local social post (A$20 boost, 1 hour). It rejects paid search because it needs a budget of at least A$200 to learn anything within 14 days.','Choose two channels and write a one-page plan with a reason, budget and time needed for each.','The plan names two channels, a budget for each and a reason based on the audience.','Why start with only two channels?','You can focus, learn what works and avoid spreading effort too thin.'),
  M('Content calendar','Create',16,'A calendar makes sure the message goes out regularly and gives you time to prepare. It also lets someone else see the plan.','List each post or email with the date, channel, format and who prepares it.|Plan a steady rhythm, such as three posts per week and one email in each week.|Mix formats, such as a photo, a short video and a customer story, while keeping one main message.|Add a review date halfway through to decide what to change.|Prepare content at least two days ahead and have a second person check it.','A two-week calendar has 6 social posts (3 each week), 2 emails (Monday of each week), and one halfway review on day 7. That is 8 pieces of content, and 5 of them are prepared by day 3.','Create a two-week calendar with dates, channels, formats, owners and a review date.','The calendar has every piece of content with a date and an owner, and a review date.','Why use a content calendar?','It keeps the work steady and organised and shows the plan to others.'),
  M('Run and monitor','Apply',24,'Running the campaign means publishing on time, answering people and watching the numbers each day. Small adjustments during the campaign can make a big difference.','Publish each item at the planned time and check that links and prices are correct.|Reply to comments and messages within one working day, because slow replies lose customers.|Record the numbers each day, such as reach, clicks and bookings, in a simple table.|If a post does badly after three days, change the picture or wording and note the change.|Keep screenshots or notes so you can explain what happened.','On day 4 a post has a reach of 300 and 3 clicks, a rate of 1%. A rewrite with a photo of a real class lifts the next post to a reach of 320 and 16 clicks, which is 5%. The log records both versions and the date of the change.','Run your campaign for two weeks with a daily log of posts, replies and numbers, and record at least one adjustment.','The log has an entry for every day, replies within one day and at least one change with its result.','Why reply to customers quickly?','Quick replies build trust and help turn interest into bookings.'),
  M('Measure','Analyse',20,'Numbers tell you what worked. You compare results with your goal and calculate cost per result to decide where to put effort next.','Collect reach, clicks and bookings for each channel for the whole campaign.|Click rate equals clicks divided by reach, and booking rate equals bookings divided by clicks.|Cost per booking equals money spent divided by bookings.|Compare channels, and say which met the goal and which did not.|Note anything that could affect the result, such as a public holiday or a price change.','A campaign spends A$60 and gets 10 bookings, so the cost per booking is 60 / 10 = A$6. Reach was 2,000, with 80 clicks, a click rate of 4%. Of those 80 clicks, 10 booked, a booking rate of 12.5%.','Build a results table with reach, clicks, bookings and cost per booking for each channel, and compare it with your goal.','All calculations are correct, and the table states whether the goal was met and what could have affected the result.','What is the cost per booking for A$60 and 10 bookings?','A$6, because 60 divided by 10 is 6.'),
  M('Privacy and ethics','Evaluate',12,'Marketing uses people attention and data, so it must respect their choices. Honest ads and consent protect both the audience and the business.','Only email people who have agreed to hear from the business, and keep a record of when they agreed.|Include a working unsubscribe link in every email and honour the request quickly.|Do not buy contact lists or use personal data for a purpose people did not agree to.|Make sure photos, quotes and reviews are real and used with permission.|Check with your local authority and an adviser for the marketing and privacy rules where you work.','A mailing list of 400 contains 60 people who never agreed. The team removes them and sends to the 340 who did. Each email has an unsubscribe link, and 4 people use it, so they are removed from the list within one day.','Check your campaign against a consent and honesty checklist and write a short note on any change you made.','The checklist covers consent, unsubscribe, honest claims and permissions, with at least one finding recorded.','Why include an unsubscribe link?','It respects consent and is usually required by law.'),
  M('Review','Evaluate',6,'The review turns results into a better next campaign. You decide what to repeat, change or stop.','Compare the results with your goal and calculate the difference in numbers.|Rank the channels by cost per booking and note which gave the most bookings.|Write three changes you will make next time and the reason for each.|Move budget toward what worked and away from what did not.|Share a short summary with the business owner and ask for feedback.','Email cost A$1.25 per booking and social cost A$8 per booking. For the next two weeks the budget of A$60 moves from 50% email and 50% social to 80% email, which is A$48, and 20% social, which is A$12.','Present a short review with results, three changes and a revised budget to the business partner or your peer circle.','The review states whether the goal was met, gives three changes with reasons and shows the new budget adding up to the total.','What should you do with a channel that works well?','Repeat it and move more effort or budget to it.')])
S('AU-07','Retail and Premium Goods Operations','Run a small premium-quality retail display and service with excellent customer care, careful stock control and honest selling. You use real counts and simple numbers to see what is working.',
 'Run a two-week pop-up or counter in a partner space with a display plan, daily stock counts, a written service standard, honest selling and a sales log, then report results and improvements.','About 120 hours over 3 to 5 months. You need a partner space, a small amount of stock, a free spreadsheet program, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Display plan with photo; daily stock count with match rate; written service standard observed in role-play; sales log with conversion, average sale and returns; customer feedback; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain what premium customers value.','Run a counter with accurate stock control.','Analyse sales, conversion and stock loss.','Create a service standard.'),
 ['Retail associate','Visual merchandiser assistant','Stock controller','Customer experience assistant'],
 [M('The premium customer','Understand',10,'Customers who buy premium goods expect quality, honest knowledge and respect for their time. Understanding this shapes how you display, speak and sell.','They value quality materials, careful finish and a product that lasts.|They expect staff to know the product and answer questions honestly, including when the answer is that you do not know.|They dislike pressure, so explain care and use and let them decide.|They value their time, so greet them without delay and avoid long, unneeded talk.|Ask what they need and what they already own before you suggest anything.','A customer asks about a leather bag. The associate explains the leather type, how to clean it and the expected life of 5 years, then says the bag is not water-proof. The customer values the honest detail and buys it.','Write a short profile of a premium customer, with three values and an example of how you would respond to each.','The profile has three values, a behaviour for each and one example, and a peer agrees it is realistic.','Name one thing premium customers value.','Quality and trust, supported by honest product knowledge.'),
  M('Display and layout','Create',16,'A display guides the eye, tells a story and makes it easy to see and touch the goods. A clear layout helps the customer decide.','Choose one focal point at eye level, such as your best item, and make it the first thing seen.|Group items in threes or by use, such as a bag, a wallet and a scarf, so customers see how they go together.|Leave space around each group so the items feel valuable and not crowded.|Use steady light that shows true colour and avoid glare on glossy items.|Keep prices clear, shown as the full price including GST where it applies, and in the same place on every item.','A table of 10 items is changed to 3 groups of 3 items plus one focal item on a stand. In the next two days, the table draws 18 stops compared with 11 before, which is 7 more.','Plan and build a display for your counter, draw the layout and take a photo, then count stops over two days.','The layout has a focal point, groups, clear prices and a photo, and the stop count is recorded.','Why use a focal point in a display?','It draws the eye first and helps tell the story of the display.'),
  M('Service standard','Create',16,'A written standard makes sure every customer gets the same care, whoever serves them. It sets what to do, how quickly and in what tone.','Greet each customer within 10 seconds of them arriving, with eye contact and a smile.|Ask open questions about what they are looking for, then listen before offering anything.|Help by showing two or three suitable items instead of everything, and explain the key difference.|Thank each customer, whether or not they buy, and offer to answer any later questions.|Follow up where agreed, such as a message after a special order within 2 days.','The standard says: greet in 10 seconds, ask one question, show 2 to 3 items, thank at the end. In a role-play of 10 customers, the associate greets 9 within 10 seconds (90%) and asks a question to all 10.','Write a one-page service standard and role-play five customer visits while a peer scores each step.','The standard has four steps with a time or number and the peer score shows how many steps were met.','What are four steps in a basic service standard?','Greet, listen, help and thank.'),
  M('Stock control','Apply',20,'Knowing exactly what you have prevents lost sales and hidden losses. Counting regularly shows where stock disappears.','Count every item at the start and end of each day and record the numbers in one sheet.|When goods arrive, check them against the delivery note and note any damage or shortage.|Opening stock plus received minus sold should equal the expected closing stock.|The difference between expected and counted stock is shrink, which comes from error, damage or theft.|Investigate any gap of more than 2% and fix the cause, such as unrecorded damaged goods.','Opening stock is 200 units, 30 received and 54 sold, so expected closing is 200 + 30 - 54 = 176 units. The count is 172, which is 4 units short. Shrink is 4 / 176 = 2.3%, above the 2% limit, so the team checks the sales records and finds 2 damaged items were removed without being recorded. The match rate (counted divided by expected) is 172 / 176 = 97.7%.','Count stock daily for two weeks, record opening, received, sold, expected and counted and calculate the match rate.','The sheet is complete for each day, the maths is correct and any gap above 2% has a written cause.','What is shrink?','Stock lost between what you should have and what you count, from error, damage or theft.'),
  M('Honest selling','Apply',16,'Trust is the base of premium retail. Honest selling means true claims, fair prices and care advice that helps the customer keep the product well.','Say only what is true, such as the real stock level, and never invent a deadline or a shortage.|Show a fair price and explain what is included, such as the warranty or the care kit.|If an item has a flaw, point it out and offer a fair adjustment.|Give care advice so the product lasts, even if it means the customer buys less often.|Check with your local authority for rules about pricing, discounts and consumer guarantees.','A customer asks if a scarf is the last one. The real count is 3, so the associate says: we have three left. A salesperson who said only one left would break the honesty rule, and a customer who found out would lose trust and might ask for a refund.','Practise five honest-selling scenarios with a peer and note which true statements you used in each.','A peer confirms that every claim was true and that care advice was given in each scenario.','Why must selling claims be true?','Trust is the core of premium retail, and false claims break consumer law.'),
  M('Run the counter','Apply',24,'Running the counter puts everything together. You record each day so that you can see what happened and improve.','Open with a standard checklist covering display, stock count, till and signage.|Log each day the number of visitors, number of sales, total sales value and any incidents.|Note customer comments, good or bad, in the customers own words.|Close with a stock count and a short summary of the day.|Handle any problem or complaint using your service standard and record how it was settled.','On one day there are 50 visits and 11 sales, so conversion is 11 / 50 = 22%. Total sales are A$528, so the average sale is 528 / 11 = A$48. One comment reads that the labels were hard to read, so the labels are enlarged the next day.','Run the counter for two weeks and keep a daily log with visits, sales, value, comments and incidents.','The log has an entry for every open day, with conversion and average sale calculated and at least one action taken from a comment.','What is the conversion for 11 sales and 50 visits?','22%, because 11 divided by 50 is 0.22.'),
  M('Read the numbers','Analyse',12,'Numbers show where to act. A few simple measures tell you if you need more visitors, better conversion or larger sales.','Average sale equals total sales divided by the number of sales.|Items per sale equals total items sold divided by the number of sales.|Return rate equals returned items divided by items sold, as a percentage.|Compare the days and look for patterns, such as higher conversion on weekdays.|Choose the weakest number and plan one change for it.','Over 10 sales, the total is A$480, so the average sale is A$48. Items sold are 18, so items per sale is 1.8. Two items are returned, a return rate of 2 / 18 = 11%. The weakest number is items per sale, so the plan is to display a matching item next to the till.','Build a table of average sale, items per sale, conversion and return rate from your two-week log and name the weakest number.','All four measures are correct, and the weakest one has a stated change.','What is the average sale for A$480 over 10 sales?','A$48, because 480 divided by 10 is 48.'),
  M('Review','Evaluate',6,'The review shows what to keep and what to improve. It also gives your partner clear information about the trial.','Collect your main results: conversion, average sale, stock match rate and customer feedback.|Compare each result with the standard and note the biggest gap.|Write three lessons and one change for each.|Share a one-page summary with your partner and ask for feedback.|Choose the one weakest number and set a target for the next trial.','The weakest number is items per sale at 1.8. The plan is to add a small gift-wrap service and display matching items, with a target of 2.2 items per sale in the next two weeks, which would raise the average sale from A$48 to about A$52 if each extra item is A$10.','Present your results, three lessons and one target to your partner or your peer circle.','The summary has four numbers, three lessons and a target with a date.','What should you do with the weakest number?','Plan one specific change and set a target to improve it in the next trial.')],
 safety="Learning only. Use a partner space and small stock; follow the space owner rules. Check with your local authority about trading, pricing and consumer guarantee rules before any real selling.")
